{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791818050,"nonce":"7gez+j6mlyclwORFtQqWkGIxlIE=","message":"AshUI comparison (2026-10-12), prompted by its lack of observed sustained first-frame failures. Read-only audit of AshUI 6aaaf9f WindowedApp.run finds three concrete differences: adapter/device initialization before Window.open, window.focus by default unless config.active=false, and a direct Window.poll/wait loop rather than the Node external libuv pump. The device-first comment explicitly says Ash await does not wake once a window is open. The focus change originated in 730bc441 to activate terminal-launched apps; that history is not evidence that focus resolves this SDK surface-refusal issue.\n\nCompiled an isolated minimal real AshUI WindowedApp using its existing ABI binary and SDK-matched xgpu/window adapters already built for the direct first-frame investigation. No AshUI, xwindow, xgpu or SDK production sources were edited; /private/tmp/ashui-order-abi remains untouched. AshUI uses hybrid --preset application. Six fresh AshUI processes vary active=true/false. SDK controls use the same real device and descriptor with ownership transferred into the normal host path: device before or after Window.open, focus on/off immediately after open, bridge versus polling. Three fresh processes per SDK configuration, 24 total. Trial order reverses on alternate runs. All 30 presented; no historical 60-second stall.\n\nAshUI experiences the same ordinary native exposure gap: all six frame logs record two failed acquisitions before presentation, and the first successful event turn spends 69.5-105.2ms in window poll/wait. First frames after scene construction are 128-175ms. Thus healthy AshUI startup does not imply every native acquisition initially succeeds, nor that the first AppKit pass is instantaneous.\n\nThe SDK device-first control removes the asynchronous result overlap with the native pump. With the bridge and default open-first ordering, native device signal to JS handler median is 84.03ms without focus and 104.90ms with focus in this batch. Device-first reduces those medians to 0.322ms and 1.526ms, respectively. Native post-open pumping still takes roughly 91-95ms in the device-first bridge controls; it merely happens after GPU completion has been delivered. Device-first does not remove the platform exposure work. Focus alone does not consistently improve SDK first-frame timing and every variant recovers, so it is not established as the historical failure's cause or fix.\n\nFirst-frame totals vary substantially on this desktop: SDK configuration medians 450-585ms, AshUI active median 603ms and inactive median 710ms. Workloads and render setup differ; these numbers are diagnostic observations, not a competitive renderer benchmark. The earlier Node-only batch was faster overall, so comparisons across those batches are not controlled. GPU completion and native pump timestamps are the useful within-batch evidence.\n\nArtifacts at /private/tmp/blinc-ashui-startup: StartupCompare.hx and .hl, staged HDLLs, comparison-runs/results.json and summary.json, per-process logs and AshUI frame TSVs. SDK control source is /private/tmp/blinc-first-frame-startup/ashui-startup-order.mjs. One initial control smoke failed before opening a window because enum constants were read from the NativeBindings class bundle; corrected to the generated gpu module before the 30 measured runs. AshUI source/ABI builds and SDK sources remain unchanged.\n\nThis makes AshUI a useful comparator for the SDK integration boundary. It confirms a way to avoid normal GPU promise-delivery delay, but there was no persistent failure in either implementation during this comparison. Keep 748f2d7 open and correlate AshUI and native SDK AppKit/bridge state during the same future no-show period before claiming a fix.","files":null}]}